Extension API: aim an injected click where the caller said, and let
that position survive until the input is consumed.

This is `doc/orion2re_open_fixes.md` item 3, BOTH halves. They are one
patch because either alone leaves the click broken: the first puts the
event on the right pixel, the second stops the real mouse from pulling
the pointer off it before the game looks.

STATUS: needed for any injected click at all on a window that is not
exactly 640x480 (first half), and for any injected click whose handler
reads the POINTER rather than the event (second half). Together they
are what the colony summary's population move waits on.

    APPLIED LOCALLY on 4 September 2026, by decision, to the
    orion2re working tree at ~/orion2re (engine 1.60.0, git
    cf4d9617). Not a silent change: it is recorded here and under
    item 3 of doc/orion2re_open_fixes.md, and it comes back off
    with one line —

        cd ~/orion2re && patch -R -p1 < \
            ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_inject_click.patch

    ONLY ONE OF THE THREE FILES IS JOES'. `src/ext/` is the
    Extension API's own directory and is untracked in his tree, so
    the ext_api.h and ext_api.cpp hunks are edits to our own code
    and diverge from nothing. `src/game/platform.cpp` is his, and
    that single hunk is the whole local divergence — the thing that
    either conflicts visibly at the next update or vanishes
    silently. The symptom of it vanishing is the population move
    going quiet again in exactly the way it did before.

    THE HUNKS BELOW ARE GENERATED, not hand-written: the edits
    were made and then diffed, so the line numbers are the file's
    own. An earlier hand-written version of this patch would not
    have applied.

FIRST HALF — THE CLICK IS AIMED IN THE WRONG SPACE

    `MSG_INJECT_CLICK` pushed its point straight into the SDL event:

        down.button.x = (float)cmd.param1;      ext_api.cpp:220

    and the game reads every mouse event back through
    `Map_Window_Point_To_Game_Point_` (platform.cpp:219), which calls
    `SDL_RenderCoordinatesFromWindow` — WINDOW space to logical
    640x480 space. So the caller's game coordinates were consumed as
    window coordinates. On a 1828 px window, (510, 326) arrives near
    (178, 114).

    The comment directly above that line already said *"Push a mouse
    click at (x, y) in 640x480 space"*. The fix makes the code keep
    the promise the comment was already making, by calling the exact
    inverse of the function the event will be read through:

        SDL_RenderCoordinatesToWindow(renderer, x, y, &wx, &wy)

    reached through `platform::g_main_window` (platform.h:31) and
    `SDL_GetRenderer`. Being the inverse rather than a scale factor is
    the point: letterboxing and non-integer scales come out right
    without anyone computing them, and asking the renderer at the
    moment of the click is the only way to be current, because the
    window is `SDL_WINDOW_RESIZABLE` and shown and can change size
    between one click and the next.

    Why a client cannot do this itself: the window size is on no wire
    message, and it is not generally measurable from outside either —
    under GNOME on Wayland, `xdotool` and `xwininfo` see no window,
    `org.gnome.Shell.Eval` is refused unless unsafe-mode is on, and
    `Shell.Introspect.GetWindows` answers AccessDenied.

    `MSG_CANCEL_FIELD` gets the same conversion. It builds its point
    from a field rect, which is in game space, so it had the identical
    defect reached through a different door.

    If the renderer cannot be reached the point passes through
    unchanged — the previous behaviour, not a silent zero.

SECOND HALF — THE POINTER DOES NOT SURVIVE THE FRAME

    `COLSUM::Get_Selected_Pop_` (colsum.cpp:1006) resolves which pop
    icon was clicked from `mouse::Pointer_X_()`, not from the click
    point. Aiming correctly is therefore not enough on its own:
    measured 4 September 2026, twice, at a 640x480 window where the
    first half is a no-op, the click reached the right FIELD and still
    selected no icon.

    "Carry the position with the event" is already how the engine
    works and is not the missing piece:

        Enqueue_Mouse_Input_Event_(x, y, buttons)   platform.cpp:132
            carries the position in the queued event
        mouse::Pointer_X_()                        mouse.cpp:51
            calls Pump_Game_Input_Queue_() FIRST, then reads
        User_Mouse_Handler_                        mouse.cpp:237
            sets the logical position FROM the event

    What defeats it is `Sync_Mouse_State_From_SDL_` (platform.cpp:825),
    which runs every frame while the window has focus and does two
    things:

        Set_Present_Mouse_Position_(real_x, real_y)   :846
        Enqueue_Mouse_Input_Event_(real_x, real_y, …) :852
            when the real position differs from the logical one

    and the second one is the sharp edge, because
    `Enqueue_Mouse_Input_Event_` COALESCES: if the last queued event is
    a mouse event with the same button state, it overwrites that
    event's coordinates rather than appending (platform.cpp:138-147).
    The injected button-up carries buttons = 0; so does the sync's
    position event. The sync therefore rewrites the injected release's
    coordinates to wherever the physical mouse is.

    When the queue is finally pumped, the press is handled at the
    injected point — which is why the right field is reached — and the
    release then moves the logical pointer to the real mouse. By the
    time `Get_Selected_Pop_` asks, the pointer has walked away.

    The fix suppresses the sync while injected input is unconsumed.
    Deterministic rather than timed: the flag is set when a click is
    injected and cleared when the input queue drains, so the
    suppression lasts exactly as long as the injected events are in
    flight and not one frame longer.

PRICE

    While suppressed, the game does not see real mouse movement or
    real button changes. That window is the one or two frames between
    injecting and pumping. It cannot leak: the clear is in
    `Pump_Game_Input_Queue_`, which is called by `Pointer_X_()`,
    `Pointer_Y_()` and the main loop, so any code that asks about the
    mouse at all ends the suppression. A player moving the physical
    mouse during those frames loses that movement.

    The alternative — tagging queued events with their origin and
    refusing to coalesce across origins — is a bigger change to a hot
    path, and it would still leave `Set_Present_Mouse_Position_` at
    :846 overwriting the position outright.

NOT A SOLUTION, AND DELIBERATELY NOT USED

    `Sync_Mouse_State_From_SDL_` already returns early when
    `g_window_focus_state == 0` (platform.cpp:826), so an injected
    pointer survives while the window is unfocused. Nothing on the
    wire reports focus, so a client cannot know it is in that state,
    and "inject only while unfocused" would be behaviour depending on
    something invisible. It explains why an injected click can appear
    to work intermittently; it is not a way to live with the bug.

    Setting the window to 640x480 is not a solution either. Under
    Wayland it is reachable only by restarting, it has to be redone
    every session, and no one can measure whether it is still true —
    a state nobody can check is a state that goes quietly missing.

